Stateless (no database)Provisioned (database row) <pre><code>KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONING=false
</code></pre> <p><code>$request-&gt;user()</code> is a <code>Bhry98\KeycloakAuth\Auth\KeycloakUser</code> built from the token claims. This is the fastest option and fits microservices that don't own user data.</p> <pre><code>$user = $request-&gt;user();
$user-&gt;id;                 // "sub"
$user-&gt;email;
$user-&gt;preferred_username; // any claim is readable as a property
$user-&gt;hasRole('admin');
$user-&gt;token();            // KeycloakToken
</code></pre> <pre><code>KEYCLOAK_PROVISIONING=true   # default
</code></pre> <p>The local user (your <code>users_model</code>) is created on the first request and updated when the claims change. While the claims stay the same, the write is skipped for <code>provisioning.sync_ttl</code> seconds, so a hot request costs a single primary-key lookup. Existing rows with the same e-mail are adopted.</p> <p>Map extra claims to columns with <code>provisioning.attributes</code>.</p> <p>Service accounts (client-credentials tokens) are never written to the database. They always resolve to a <code>KeycloakUser</code>, so machine clients work even with provisioning on.</p>

Event Payload Typical use <code>TokenValidated</code> <code>$token</code>, <code>$guard</code> metrics, tenant context <code>TokenValidationFailed</code> <code>$exception</code>, <code>$guard</code>, <code>$request</code> audit log, alerting on forged tokens <code>UserLoggedIn</code> <code>$user</code>, <code>$token</code>, <code>$guard</code> last-login timestamp, welcome flow <code>UserLoggedOut</code> <code>$user</code>, <code>$guard</code> audit log <code>TokenRefreshed</code> <code>$token</code>, <code>$guard</code> refresh cached permissions <code>UserProvisioned</code> <code>$user</code>, <code>$token</code>, <code>$created</code> sync roles / teams, welcome e-mail on <code>$created</code> <pre><code>Event::listen(UserProvisioned::class, function (UserProvisioned $event) {
    if ($event-&gt;created) {
        Mail::to($event-&gt;user)-&gt;queue(new Welcome);
    }
});
</code></pre> <p>All events live in <code>Bhry98\KeycloakAuth\Events</code>.</p>
